It seems like you're working on a Vue.js project and need help with creating a reusable component that can handle various data types and be used in multiple contexts. Let's break down the steps to create such a component.

### Step 1: Define the Component Structure

Create a new Vue component file, for example, `ReusableComponent.vue`.

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <!-- Render content based on the type of data -->
    <div v-if="type === 'text'">{{ data }}</div>
    <div v-else-if="type === 'image'">
      <img :src="data" alt="Image" />
    </div>
    <div v-else-if="type === 'list'">
      <ul>
        <li v-for="(item, index) in data" :key="index">{{ item }}</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <!-- Add more conditions for other data types -->
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  name: 'ReusableComponent',
  props: {
    type: {
      type: String,
      required: true
    },
    data: {
      type: [String, Array, Object],
      required: true
    }
  }
}
</script>

<style scoped>
/* Add component-specific styles here */
</style>
```

### Step 2: Use the Component in Your Application

Now, you can use this component in any part of your Vue application. For example, in a parent component:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <ReusableComponent type="text" data="Hello, world!" />
    <ReusableComponent type="image" data="https://example.com/image.jpg" />
    <ReusableComponent type="list" :data="['Item 1', 'Item 2', 'Item 3']" />
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import ReusableComponent from '@/components/ReusableComponent.vue';

export default {
  components: {
    ReusableComponent
  }
}
</script>
```

### Step 3: Extend the Component for More Data Types

If you need to handle more complex data types, you can extend the component. For example, to handle a custom object type:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <!-- Existing conditions -->
    <div v-else-if="type === 'customObject'">
      <div v-for="(value, key) in data" :key="key">
        <strong>{{ key }}:</strong> {{ value }}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  // Existing props
  props: {
    type: {
      type: String,
      required: true
    },
    data: {
      type: [String, Array, Object],
      required: true
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

### Step 4: Add Event Handling

If you need to emit events from the component, you can add methods and emit events accordingly. For example:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <button @click="handleClick">Click Me</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  // Existing props
  props: {
    type: {
      type: String,
      required: true
    },
    data: {
      type: [String, Array, Object],
      required: true
    }
  },
  methods: {
    handleClick() {
      this.$emit('customEvent', { message: 'Button clicked' });
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

### Step 5: Use the Component with Event Handling

In the parent component, you can listen to the emitted event:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <ReusableComponent type="text" data="Hello, world!" @customEvent="handleCustomEvent" />
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import ReusableComponent from '@/components/ReusableComponent.vue';

export default {
  components: {
    ReusableComponent
  },
  methods: {
    handleCustomEvent(event) {
      console.log(event.message);
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

### Summary

- **ReusableComponent.vue**: A flexible component that handles different data types.
- **Parent component**: Uses the reusable component and handles events.

